===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|business}} Anything movable (a [[good]]) that is [[bought]] and [[sold]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Polanyi Great Transformation|chapter=6|page=72|passage=It is with the help of the '''commodity''' concept that the mechanism of the market is geared to the various elements of industrial life. '''Commodities''' are here empirically defined as  objects produced for sale on the market; {{...}}. But labor, land, and money are obviously ''not'' '''commodities'''; the postulate that anything that is bought and sold must have been produced for sale is emphatically untrue in regard to them.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|author=James G. Carrier|title=Gifts and Commodities: Exchange and Western Capitalism Since 1700|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=7BwbwuPyLccC&pg=PA122&dq=commodities+shopping&sig=dozEQAUipFYsEyUTkKpSqKbSCqk|page=122
|passage=If a key part of shopping is the conversion of anonymous '''commodities''' into possessions, shopping is a cultural as much as an economic activity.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=Rachel Pain|title=Introducing Social Geographies|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=FVb2cfVpST8C&pg=PA26&dq=commodities+shopping&sig=OhV4OSNFSU9DE2-UdUBQsFosh5Y|page=26
|passage=In human geography "'''commodities'''" usually refers to goods and services which are bought and sold. The simplest '''commodities''' are those produced by the production system just before they are sold.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=William Leiss, Botterill, Jacki|title=Social Communication in Advertising: Consumption in the Mediated Marketplace|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=R_ELAoKRDb4C&pg=PA307&dq=commodities+shopping&sig=AyH9Brabe3RC16bitD6AOsfSfPw|page=307|text=Referring to the work of Bourdieu, Zukin (2004,38) notes that shopping is much more than the purchase of '''commodities'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023 March 8|author=Gareth Dennis|title=The Reshaping of things to come...|journal=RAIL|issue=978|page=48|text=For mineral trains, he adds little other than pointing out the need to expand single-'''commodity''' trains and to end the use of mixed-'''commodity''' services that required extensive marshalling.}}
# Something [[useful]] or [[valuable]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2008 January 14|work=Somerset County Gazette|text=And Slade said: "It really makes me sad that football club chairmen and boards seem to have lost that most precious '''commodity''' - patience. "Sam's sacking at Newcastle had, I suppose, been on the cards for a while, but it is really ridiculous to fire a manager after such a short time.}}
# {{lb|en|economics}} [[raw material|Raw materials]], [[agricultural]] and other [[primary]] products as objects of [[large-scale]] trading in specialized [[exchanges]].
#: {{ux|en|The price of crude oil is determined in continuous trading between professional players in World's many '''commodities''' exchanges.}}
# {{lb|en|marketing}} [[undifferentiated|Undifferentiated]] goods characterized by a low [[profit margin]], as distinguished from [[brand]]ed products.
#: {{ux|en|Although they were once in the forefront of consumer electronics, the calculators have become a mere '''commodity'''.}}
# {{lb|en|Marxism}} Anything which has both a [[use value]] and an [[exchange value]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[convenience|Convenience]]; [[usefulness]], [[suitability]]. {{defdate|15th|19th c.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[self-interest|Self-interest]]; personal [[convenience]] or [[advantage]]. {{defdate|16th|19th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes|chapter=40|book=I|passage=Shall we employ the intelligence Heaven hath bestowed upon us for our greatest good, to our ruine? repugning natures desseign and the universal order and vicissitude of things, which implieth that every man should use his instruments and meanes for his owne '''commoditie'''?}}
#* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy|edition=2nd|passage=they commonly respect their own ends, '''commodity''' is the steer of all their action{{nb...}}.}}

